tion plus how it supports the claim"],"question":"Which record best supports a science claim about computer?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"The strongest choice applies the taught idea about computer with evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Thinking computers “know” things without data/programs anthropomorphises them","Change several conditions at once so something happens faster","Ignore measurements and decide by which looks nicer","Input; output; stored data (also an output when shown)"],"question":"Using this science idea — A computer is a device that takes inputs, follows instructions and produces outputs. — which choice is strongest?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Fair tests change one variable, control the rest, and use recorded evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Change several conditions at once so something happens faster","Stop early when you like the first result","Ignore measurements and decide by which looks nicer","Change one variable and keep the other conditions the same"],"question":"Which investigation design best tests an idea about computer?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Scientific predictions stay open to new observations rather than protecting a first guess. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["If the observed feature changes, the claim about computer should change with it","Any exciting story about the object must be true","Thinking computers “know” things without data/programs anthropomorphises them","Results that disagree with the first guess should be discarded"],"question":"Which prediction about computer can be checked with evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept a new example with named observations and a claim–evidence link about computer. Reject title-only labels with no observation.","question":"Investigate or describe a fresh example (not the worked objects) that uses computer. Record evidence and explain how it supports your claim.","sample_answer":"Sample: new object/context, two named observations, claim about computer linked to that evidence.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception plus corrected claim and evidence reason for computer. Reject wording changes that leave the same error.","question":"Name one science misconception about computer, correct it with evidence, and say why the correction works.","sample_answer":"Sample misconception: Thinking computers “know” things without data/programs anthropomorphises them.. Correction: Input; output; stored data (also an output when shown). with evidence.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-sci-y3-w26-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA Science and Technology alignment using catalogue outcome codes; content-point mapping remains pending.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise computer.","Read the mission before you start observing or testing.","Keep the property or variable you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the observation or step that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore observations or fair tests.","Prefer accurate ev