y the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nInput; output; stored data (also an output when shown).","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific science error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen or measured.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Thinking computers “know” things without data/programs anthropomorphises them. Correct the claim or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use the same science idea on a new object, material or context, not a copy of the worked example.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","Use the same science idea on a new object, material or context, not a copy of the worked example","A guess with no evidence","A claim unrelated to the observation or test"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about computer with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA computer is a device that takes inputs, follows instructions and produces outputs. Data is information (numbers, words, images) computers store and use. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInvestigate or describe 